#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124329 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/id #uuid "f7b85641-289b-57b2-9ffc-ff4c5e10025a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8572" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=3.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $A-3I\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124330 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "854f39a0-99b3-59f3-a9d7-4909850254ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-8573" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=5$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1= -{2}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nBy setting $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-2}{1} = -2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124331 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "cb76e9dc-1195-58d3-8e58-a3421770265a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8574" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm. \n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=3, \\quad\\quad\\lambda_2=-2.\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the corresponding system of linear equations, we construct the augmented matrix $M$ and reduce it to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{8}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). So, $x_1$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix corresponds to a homogeneous system of linear equations. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). So, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=\\dfrac{8}{5}x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{5}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting  $x_2=5,$ we get the eigenvector  $[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124332 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "7959b6aa-7ef0-5ba1-b74b-5e59481d0145" :question/math-academy-id "e-8575" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ You're given that $\\lambda_1=4$ and $\\lambda_2=3$ are the eigenvalues of $A,$ and\n$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PDP^{-1} = A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4}$ and $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3},$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}4},$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58cceb92-58fe-5156-989f-0b2a057fc8bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306214} {:answer/id #uuid "62b3a655-cb21-5490-b639-285b81db3c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306215} {:answer/id #uuid "a848b508-0c63-53ba-aa21-33ee30a038f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306216} {:answer/id #uuid "ee9dd13c-21b2-53d9-bd93-6975ce578bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306217} {:answer/id #uuid "8834a7c9-ee24-5c0d-9840-34fb22108046" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9dd13c-21b2-53d9-bd93-6975ce578bda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306217} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf1b537c-5521-5da0-8038-c727415e4ebf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306213}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d92b551-0b27-59ea-861b-c8ea209a5b0c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140041" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -3 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-(1)(-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +5+3 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27c01e01-7ee8-52e8-8d9b-68aed4d3b740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306221} {:answer/id #uuid "fb766637-1864-5ffe-9c7e-2a25126f2065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306222} {:answer/id #uuid "40b54c04-fd69-5a26-ab3f-9500095b483d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306223} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c3bb3a-be59-5524-b6e9-d5f530c3b052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306224} {:answer/id #uuid "7917d3fe-72ff-5aaf-8d0a-8364bfed0a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb766637-1864-5ffe-9c7e-2a25126f2065" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306222} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7a191dc-0130-5fb7-9b64-41f161a6b503" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8ec40444-64f8-58a7-b09c-d9c17582ccae" :question/math-academy-id "q-140055" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{4}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ff6446e-7913-5d55-adfd-124649f3f1c0" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cab45579-9d99-5602-a5de-7ab45da25216" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306228} {:answer/id #uuid "16924c86-72f6-53f8-8e7c-719d02b7ca89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306229} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d92175-fa2e-5cf3-83f5-e82cf225cc2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306230} {:answer/id #uuid "e781750e-f339-5376-aa76-feca24822b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306231} {:answer/id #uuid "0f306b0d-86a2-5b31-b6fc-98874112414e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306232}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e781750e-f339-5376-aa76-feca24822b28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306231} :answer-field/id #uuid "20837c8e-968e-53ae-9a41-e4fb54728aea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "452b1c45-f344-59d0-8f2d-8526fb787055" :question/math-academy-id "q-36914" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$, \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix. Since matrix $A$ is triangular, its eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a homogeneous system of linear equations with the matrix as above. Note, the matrix is already in row echelon form. There is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. On the other hand, from the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fd241e-b38c-55d8-9f12-a12d08a0e16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338919} {:answer/id #uuid "353e424e-0ac2-568e-ae87-ff7bcb33a26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338920} {:answer/id #uuid "4de4da56-2e52-5786-8a37-4434e69b56db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338921} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d0f0fa-3911-5761-bd3c-7b85a16c8165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338922} {:answer/id #uuid "a792d964-068d-5f40-a5fd-e768ca2cf81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a792d964-068d-5f40-a5fd-e768ca2cf81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} :answer-field/id #uuid "c82c89df-a0fb-5a2f-8956-b3be5f6ac309" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c022e488-b03e-52fb-b544-84b03490d40c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36929" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is triangular, the eigenvalues are the entries on the main diagonal. \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=4.$ Since we get one repeated eigenvalue, the matrix might not be diagonalizable. To check this, we row-reduce the matrix $(A-4I)\\text{x}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has only $1$ pivot column. This implies that we will have only $2-1=1$ linearly independent eigenvector corresponding to the current eigenvalue. Therefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4f0d46-6560-5fdc-afa7-c2f956e198f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123035} {:answer/id #uuid "a81831d2-0500-52f1-8970-0ae679402055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123036} {:answer/id #uuid "b22b39a5-ec27-5900-8399-263a2463a846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123037} {:answer/id #uuid "c97a4802-23bd-5920-afea-ac1d692c8542" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123038} {:answer/id #uuid "0aa8941c-8c7d-5743-a8e6-925fb3c1af2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0aa8941c-8c7d-5743-a8e6-925fb3c1af2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123039} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ad2ef77-4c05-5edf-b6c7-70f6f925dfa4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123034}] :question/id #uuid "474a90c6-ac56-574a-b515-a9ddafabb1ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-37599" :question/problem "If the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}10 & -4 \\\\ 24 & -10\\end{bmatrix}$\nis diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88847457-f4b4-5e2d-9345-dd0a9b98e9a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306281} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc469a9-7937-55be-9104-59d3272e1da7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=15,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306282} {:answer/id #uuid "31911b15-83cf-54d3-ad0b-446b9b604eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306283} {:answer/id #uuid "ab82adc1-ef0d-5502-8c8c-cfd158599c74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306284} {:answer/id #uuid "28c19d3c-2295-53e4-964b-399ff2f13b03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31911b15-83cf-54d3-ad0b-446b9b604eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306283} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d7af8e-578e-5a22-8502-5334e5c627b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306280}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52d32399-93d9-5ce5-a996-5d60c1e0900e" :question/math-academy-id "q-37610" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & b \\\\ 2 & c\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=15,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=15.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3d7cb0b-7d81-55a5-9781-dda7e9cc9fb9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given 2x2 Matrix Is Diagonalizable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e4cc75a-c249-5c14-b6c2-046927dabc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306288} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ec39a1-2872-544a-8145-aabe3cc392dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306289} {:answer/id #uuid "17065d17-4eb7-5c27-86fb-33c626b98e07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306290} {:answer/id #uuid "cd96262c-5415-5073-9f57-f40b01533bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306291} {:answer/id #uuid "246b7dcc-a1d6-532c-b83d-012038bef7de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e4cc75a-c249-5c14-b6c2-046927dabc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306288} :answer-field/id #uuid "845b12d1-c113-5f21-97cc-64bc021cfebc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a70cd34e-7f71-566e-9b48-b1d57ca344ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-37629" :question/problem "Determine if the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}]$ \nis diagonalizable or not, and if it is diagonalizable, find a corresponding diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable.\n\nNow, we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306300 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5164c6cb-aef0-554b-b361-77245991c0ea" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "629a0baa-dc7e-5342-9844-6a3d285087a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306302} {:answer/id #uuid "8b868259-d51e-56a7-b9e4-714a37a2a3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306303} {:answer/id #uuid "7645a980-b29d-5fe8-bd7c-485982bee7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306304} {:answer/id #uuid "f94b1b57-4526-5515-963d-1874737b1f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306305} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ab0081-9309-5329-9339-c7eef19c7ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f94b1b57-4526-5515-963d-1874737b1f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]$ and \n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306305} :answer-field/id #uuid "2109692d-b0e7-56cf-b33e-1e82cc41274e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fb53181-29d9-5f89-8598-3c491720e6bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-80389" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ You're given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$ are the eigenvalues of $A$, and\n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ are the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ and\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186306335 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814714a3-6ec6-54e2-9d83-6b5ab94883f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306337} {:answer/id #uuid "b30ec5a3-856c-5987-85c9-4fcddffa926e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306338} {:answer/id #uuid "400c897a-e7be-52b5-91cb-2ed277aa1e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306339} {:answer/id #uuid "6e32ce35-1563-5bd9-8946-434c332f076a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306340} {:answer/id #uuid "6bbb9458-fb18-5571-8136-c01aebced69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bbb9458-fb18-5571-8136-c01aebced69a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306341} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c1e864-5cd7-5eab-8a53-0af240d1b84d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21d5e4ed-55a5-585d-b95d-dc1afd92b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-80458" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & ∗ \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe74362-353d-5924-9358-45d39d94a180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338932} {:answer/id #uuid "0ebf8095-a684-5c4c-99ad-553456f51919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338933} {:answer/id #uuid "44eecb0a-d30c-5126-85fc-c561fe5c7b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ceb67b-95fc-5d21-a3ae-11502dc74115" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338935} {:answer/id #uuid "7892ba22-d3ed-5a02-a5bd-57153777044b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44eecb0a-d30c-5126-85fc-c561fe5c7b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338934} :answer-field/id #uuid "06fa57d8-1995-5f95-b249-3df8a454a386" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b873a918-b542-5b82-8f6e-b94782f43ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-80464" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & a \\\\ 2 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Computing $A-2I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -10\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 \\\\ 6 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}6 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca18fa52-5494-53ad-bd02-208aba4ff9e1" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a 2x2 Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3c0f626-5892-5a52-80b4-8ced8f520502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338939} {:answer/id #uuid "6a6dfb09-d3a7-5cf3-ad9d-b5db55f5153a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940} {:answer/id #uuid "e4f866f8-11c5-5336-a804-a58dc14462a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338941} {:answer/id #uuid "cb3d90b3-2aab-56a9-b56a-8990039213b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338942} {:answer/id #uuid "419fedc6-e1b5-5400-a395-35e2a75b196e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a6dfb09-d3a7-5cf3-ad9d-b5db55f5153a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338940} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dc8dfc0-386e-5fe6-a862-155dc9f936d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "912d4ab2-e55a-5e9a-8ddf-b1e59d14eb18" :question/math-academy-id "q-80467" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$   find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$  \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ \n\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$ Computing $A-3I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}]}
